{% part title="What it combines with" %}
None of the talk’s demos runs it with another stage {% own /%}.

The talk’s author used the edges as roads in a game and filled each cell with a kind of district {% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s142" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s143" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s144" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s145" /%}.
{% /part %}

{% part title="Checked against the talk’s code" %}
Our stage against the talk’s own code, each run on seeds 0 to 199 and [measured the same way](#is-this-the-talks-technique) {% own-run values="list.checked.voronoi" /%}:

- `c08a-voronoi`: 7 of 7 traits consistent with the talk’s own code.
{% /part %}

{% /card %}

{% card title="Noise" id="noise" kind="Generator" %}
Smooth random values from −1 to 1 at every point, most often read as altitude to make an overworld {% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s149" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s150" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s151" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s162" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s163" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s164" /%}.

Stage `noise`. The talk’s demos: `c10-perlin`, `c11-overworld`, `c12-noisezoom`, `c13-double-noise`.

{% part title="The rule" %}
```text
for every tile: n = fractal noise at the tile's position, from -1 to 1
height:    brightness = (n + 1) / 2
overworld: water if n < 0, grass if n < 0.5, otherwise mountain
zoom:      sample positions are multiplied by a scale that shrinks
double:    a second, finer noise is mixed in as the scale shrinks
```

The demos use fractal simplex noise, the first one too, though it is named for Perlin {% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c10-perlin/src/main.rs#L13-L15" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c11-overworld/src/main.rs#L20-L22" /%}. The talk gives the green band as altitudes from 0 to 0.75 {% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s164" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s165" /%}. Its code draws mountain from 0.5, and 0.75 is there only in the water’s color {% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c11-overworld/src/main.rs#L28-L44" /%}.
{% /part %}

{% part title="Step through it" %}
{% interactive id="lab" caption="Step through Noise: height map, Noise: overworld, Noise: zoom, Noise: double: seed 1, 80 by 50 tiles. Change the seed or a control and the map is built again." params={mode: "card", stage: "noise", demos: ["c10-perlin", "c11-overworld", "c12-noisezoom", "c13-double-noise"]} %}
{% steps %}
{% step src="assets/steps/c10-0001-light.png" dark="assets/steps/c10-0001-dark.png" alt="Noise: height map, seed 1: the map at step 1 of 5." key="noise.map" at=1 of=5 %}
Fractal simplex noise is sampled once at every tile and drawn as brightness, from 0 to 1 {% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c10-perlin/src/main.rs#L11-L26" /%}.
{% /step %}
{% step src="assets/steps/c10-0002-light.png" dark="assets/steps/c10-0002-dark.png" alt="Noise: height map, seed 1: the map at step 2 of 5." key="noise.map" at=2 of=5 %}
Fractal simplex noise is sampled once at every tile and drawn as brightness, from 0 to 1 {% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c10-perlin/src/main.rs#L11-L26" /%}.
{% /step %}
{% step src="assets/steps/c10-0003-light.png" dark="assets/steps/c10-0003-dark.png" alt="Noise: height map, seed 1: the map at step 3 of 5." key="noise.map" at=3 of=5 %}
Fractal simplex noise is sampled once at every tile and drawn as brightness, from 0 to 1 {% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c10-perlin/src/main.rs#L11-L26" /%}.
{% /step %}
{% step src="assets/steps/c10-0004-light.png" dark="assets/steps/c10-0004-dark.png" alt="Noise: height map, seed 1: the map at step 4 of 5." key="noise.map" at=4 of=5 %}
Fractal simplex noise is sampled once at every tile and drawn as brightness, from 0 to 1 {% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c10-perlin/src/main.rs#L11-L26" /%}.
{% /step %}
{% step src="assets/steps/c10-0005-light.png" dark="assets/steps/c10-0005-dark.png" alt="Noise: height map, seed 1: the map at step 5 of 5." key="noise.map" at=5 of=5 %}
Fractal simplex noise is sampled once at every tile and drawn as brightness, from 0 to 1 {% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c10-perlin/src/main.rs#L11-L26" /%}.
{% /step %}
{% step src="assets/steps/c11-0001-light.png" dark="assets/steps/c11-0001-dark.png" alt="Noise: overworld, seed 1: the map at step 1 of 1." key="noise.map#c11-overworld" at=1 of=1 %}
Noise is sampled once at every tile and read as altitude: below 0 is water, from 0 up to 0.5 grass, and 0.5 or more mountain {% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c11-overworld/src/main.rs#L20-L44" /%}.
{% /step %}
{% step src="assets/steps/c12-0001-light.png" dark="assets/steps/c12-0001-dark.png" alt="Noise: zoom, seed 1: the map at step 1 of 92." key="noise.zoomed-out" at=1 of=92 %}
The noise at scale 1, labeled “Zoomed Out”, cut into three bands by its value: the talk’s water, green terrain and mountains {% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c12-noisezoom/src/main.rs#L31-L47" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c12-noisezoom/src/main.rs#L66-L76" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s164" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s165" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s166" /%}.
{% /step %}
{% step src="assets/steps/c12-0002-light.png" dark="assets/steps/c12-0002-dark.png" alt="Noise: zoom, seed 1: the map at step 2 of 92." key="noise.scale" at=2 of=92 %}
Every tile’s sample position is multiplied by a scale that shrinks a hundredth at a time, so a smaller and smaller part of the same noise fills the map {% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c12-noisezoom/src/main.rs#L31-L33" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c12-noisezoom/src/main.rs#L77-L91" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s167" /%}. The scale, in hundredths: {% value %}100{% /value %}.
{% /step %}
{% step src="assets/steps/c12-0092-light.png" dark="assets/steps/c12-0092-dark.png" alt="Noise: zoom, seed 1: the map at step 92 of 92." key="noise.scale" at=92 of=92 %}
Every tile’s sample position is multiplied by a scale that shrinks a hundredth at a time, so a smaller and smaller part of the same noise fills the map {% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c12-noisezoom/src/main.rs#L31-L33" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c12-noisezoom/src/main.rs#L77-L91" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s167" /%}. The scale, in hundredths: {% value %}10{% /value %}.
{% /step %}
{% step src="assets/steps/c13-0001-light.png" dark="assets/steps/c13-0001-dark.png" alt="Noise: double, seed 1: the map at step 1 of 94." key="noise.first" at=1 of=94 %}
The first noise on its own, with the demo’s base settings {% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c13-double-noise/src/main.rs#L23-L29" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c13-double-noise/src/main.rs#L71-L105" /%}.
{% /step %}
{% step src="assets/steps/c13-0002-light.png" dark="assets/steps/c13-0002-dark.png" alt="Noise: double, seed 1: the map at step 2 of 94." key="noise.second" at=2 of=94 %}
The second noise on its own: another seed, half the octaves and gain, one more of lacunarity and four times the frequency {% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c13-double-noise/src/main.rs#L31-L37" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c13-double-noise/src/main.rs#L108-L135" /%}. The talk calls it much more bumpy, and terrible as a continent {% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s169" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s170" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s171" /%}.
{% /step %}
{% step src="assets/steps/c13-0003-light.png" dark="assets/steps/c13-0003-dark.png" alt="Noise: double, seed 1: the map at step 3 of 94." key="noise.zoomed-out#c13-double-noise" at=3 of=94 %}
The two noises mixed at scale 1, where the first counts in full and the second is taken away at a quarter of its strength {% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c13-double-noise/src/main.rs#L39-L44" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c13-double-noise/src/main.rs#L139-L149" /%}.
{% /step %}
{% step src="assets/steps/c13-0004-light.png" dark="assets/steps/c13-0004-dark.png" alt="Noise: double, seed 1: the map at step 4 of 94." key="noise.scale#c13-double-noise" at=4 of=94 %}
As the scale shrinks, the first noise’s weight falls with it to a half and the second’s rises to a quarter, and both stay there from a scale of 0.5 down {% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c13-double-noise/src/main.rs#L39-L44" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c13-double-noise/src/main.rs#L150-L164" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s171" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s172" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s173" /%}. The scale, in hundredths: {% value %}100{% /value %}.
{% /step %}
{% step src="assets/steps/c13-0094-light.png" dark="assets/steps/c13-0094-dark.png" alt="Noise: double, seed 1: the map at step 94 of 94." key="noise.scale#c13-double-noise" at=94 of=94 %}
As the scale shrinks, the first noise’s weight falls with it to a half and the second’s rises to a quarter, and both stay there from a scale of 0.5 down {% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c13-double-noise/src/main.rs#L39-L44" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c13-double-noise/src/main.rs#L150-L164" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s171" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s172" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s173" /%}. The scale, in hundredths: {% value %}10{% /value %}.
{% /step %}
{% /steps %}

On the command line: `map-lab build recipes/c10-perlin.json`, `map-lab build recipes/c11-overworld.json`, `map-lab build recipes/c12-noisezoom.json`, `map-lab build recipes/c13-double-noise.json`.
{% /interactive %}
{% /part %}

{% part title="Controls" %}
- `mode` (`overworld`, `height`, `zoom`, `double`; the talk’s: `height` in `c10`, `overworld` in `c11`, `zoom` in `c12`, `double` in `c13`) {% own /%}. What is made of the noise: a `height` map in gray, an `overworld` of water, grass and mountain, a `zoom` that draws the same bands at a shrinking scale, or the zoom with two noises mixed, `double` {% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c10-perlin/src/main.rs#L21-L26" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c11-overworld/src/main.rs#L28-L44" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c12-noisezoom/src/main.rs#L31-L47" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c12-noisezoom/src/main.rs#L77-L91" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c13-double-noise/src/main.rs#L39-L44" /%}.
- `octaves` (1 to 16; the talk’s: `1`, `10` in `c10`, `10` in `c11`, `3` in `c12`, `3` in `c13`) {% own /%}. In the talk’s words, how many different gradients it is mixing in {% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s154" /%}.
- `gain` (0 to 4; the talk’s: `0.2`, `0.5` in `c10`, `0.1` in `c11`, `0.005` in `c12`, `0.005` in `c13`) {% own /%}. In the talk’s words, how long the various gradients last {% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s155" /%}. In our noise, each further layer’s strength is the one before it times this {% own /%}.
- `lacunarity` (0 to 16; the talk’s: `1`, `5` in `c10`, `5` in `c11`, `4` in `c12`, `4` in `c13`) {% own /%}. In the talk’s words, it adds in randomness {% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s156" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s157" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s158" /%}. In our noise, each further layer’s frequency is the one before it times this {% own /%}.
- `frequency` (0 to 64; the talk’s: `1`, `5` in `c10`, `2` in `c11`, `0.08` in `c12`, `0.08` in `c13`) {% own /%}. In the talk’s words, how frequently each of the octaves peaks {% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s159" /%}.
{% /part %}

{% part title="What it produces, measured" %}
Seeds 0 to 199, our own runs: the median, then the range 8 of 10 seeds fall in {% own-run values="table.measured.noise" /%}.

{% table label="Measured traits: Noise" rowheads=true key=true %}
* Trait
* Noise: height map (c10)
