## How this was measured

{% callout type="note" title="These definitions are ours" %}
The talk doesn’t measure its maps. Every trait, the probe and every failure criterion below is our own definition, written for this page and applied the same way to our code and to the talk’s {% own /%}.
{% /callout %}

Each of the talk’s 22 demos is a recipe, built once on each of seeds 0 to 199 by the same core that runs in this page {% own /%}. The data file lists every value with the recipe and the seed behind it {% ref source="this" path="assets/lab/traits.json" lines="1-6" /%}.

### Our definitions

What we measure on a finished map {% own /%}:

{% table label="The traits we measure" rowheads=true %}
* Trait
* Our definition
---
* Floor share
* Walkable tiles over all tiles. Walkable: floor, grass, treasure and traps; not wall, water or mountain.
---
* Regions
* Groups of walkable tiles joined in four directions.
---
* Dead ends
* Walkable tiles with exactly one walkable neighbor of four.
---
* Corridor ratio
* The share of walkable tiles in no 2 x 2 block of walkable tiles: passages one tile wide.
---
* Path
* Steps on the shortest four-way walk from start to exit; null when there is none.
---
* Water
* Water tiles over all tiles.
---
* Grass
* Grass tiles over all tiles.
---
* Mountain
* Mountain tiles over all tiles.
---
* Rooms placed
* Rooms placed, for maps built from rooms; null otherwise.
{% /table %}

A map with no start and exit of its own is measured between the open tile nearest the middle of its left edge and the open tile nearest the middle of its right edge: the probe {% own /%}. Those two points are where the talk’s demos look for a start and for an exit {% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c17-starting-left/src/main.rs#L69-L86" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c18-ending-right/src/main.rs#L92-L109" /%}.

### What counts as failing

A seed fails a demo when its map meets one of these {% own /%}:

{% table label="What counts as failing" rowheads=true %}
* Failure
* Our criterion
---
* No place for the prefab
* A prefab stage found no place for its prefab (the talk’s loop would run forever).
---
* Exit unreachable
* The exit stage’s exit can’t be walked to from the start.
---
* No floor
* A stage needed a walkable tile and the map had none.
---
* A loop hit our cap
* A loop the talk leaves uncapped hit our cap: a particle that never lands, a prefab that never fits.
---
* Not one region
* The walkable tiles form more than one region.
---
* No path
* No walk from start to exit (the map’s own, or the standard probe’s).
---
* Short path
* Start and exit fewer than a quarter of the map’s width apart, in steps.
---
* Few rooms
* A room generator placed fewer than four rooms.
{% /table %}

### The walk

The talk suggests hiding something at the least accessible point of a map for a treasure hunt, and bonus things in the gray rooms, the ones its route doesn’t need {% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s254" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s255" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s256" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s267" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s268" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s269" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s270" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s271" /%}. The lab’s walk borrows that: caches to find on the way to the exit. The talk has no walk, and these rules are ours {% own /%}:

- Up to five caches go on floor or grass, at least the hot path’s radius from the route by walking. The first goes on the tile farthest from the route, and each one after it on the tile farthest from those already placed, more than 12 steps from them {% own /%}.
- You see up to nine tiles away in straight lines over tiles that can be walked on, and the walls that touch what you see. You remember what you have seen {% own /%}.
- Afterward the lab draws your route over the shortest one, on the map shaded by walking distance from the start {% own /%}.

### Is this the talk’s technique?

Reading the talk’s code takes one key: `.` is its solid tile and `#` its open one {% own /%}. A new map is all `.` {% cite id="wolverson2020-code/backend/src/lib.rs#L12-L16" /%}, and its pathfinding steps only onto neighbors that are not `.` {% cite id="wolverson2020-code/backend/src/lib.rs#L54-L65" /%}.

We built the talk’s code with only its window and its random seeding replaced, and ran every demo on the same 200 seeds {% own /%}. Its random number generator isn’t ours, so the maps can’t match tile for tile, and the comparison is of distributions: for each demo and trait a two-sample Kolmogorov–Smirnov test, with a trait counted as differing when p is under 0.01 {% own /%}. 182 of 183 compared traits are consistent {% own-run values="faith.consistent faith.compared table.faithfulness" /%}.

